    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0003",
      "sourceStart": 624,
      "sourceEnd": 626,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch09-item-1-1-1",
      "sourceStart": 626,
      "sourceEnd": 760,
      "original": "#### 1.1.1. <span style=\"color: red\">(Bilinear form)</span> A bilinear form is a function $\\beta : V \\times V \\rightarrow F$ such that",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0004",
      "sourceStart": 760,
      "sourceEnd": 1230,
      "sha256": "72570c3a1c4f12525ab8c3c2e0cb7b2a5d120c6ef581607359c9f95221b6aced"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch09-item-1-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 1230,
      "sourceEnd": 1503,
      "original": "#### 1.1.2. <span style=\"color: red\">(Matrix representation of bilinear form)</span> Suppose $\\beta$ is a bilinear form and let $\\mathscr{B}=(e_1,\\ldots,e_n)$ be an ordered basis of $V$. The matrix representation is defined as $M_{\\mathscr{B}}(\\beta)_{i,j}=\\beta(e_i,e_j)$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0005",
      "sourceStart": 1503,
      "sourceEnd": 1576,
      "sha256": "4e7704e66a60ca05c91d6e67c2e89009267374e9a0e43bdef29a4bc8c9a159f6"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch09-item-1-1-3",
      "sourceStart": 1576,
      "sourceEnd": 1825,
      "original": "#### 1.1.3. <span style=\"color: red\">(Quadratic form)</span> For $\\beta \\in V^{(2)}$, define $q_\\beta:V \\rightarrow F$ by $q_\\beta(v)=\\beta(v,v)$. A function $q:V\\to F$ is called a quadratic form on $V$ if there exists $\\beta$ such that $q=q_\\beta$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0006",
      "sourceStart": 1825,
      "sourceEnd": 1827,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch09-section-1-2",
      "sourceStart": 1827,
      "sourceEnd": 1865,
      "original": "### 1.2. Alternating Multilinear Forms",
      "action": "moved-to-section-title"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0007",
      "sourceStart": 1865,
      "sourceEnd": 1867,
      "sha256": "75a11da44c802486bc6f65640aa48a730f0f684c5c07a42ba3cd1735eb3fb070"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch09-item-1-2-1",
      "sourceStart": 1867,
      "sourceEnd": 2113,
      "original": "#### 1.2.1. <span style=\"color: red\">(Multilinear form)</span> An $m$-linear form on $V$ is a function $\\alpha:V^m\\to F$ that is linear in each variable while the other variables are fixed. The vector space of these forms is denoted by $V^{(m)}$.",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0008",
      "sourceStart": 2113,
      "sourceEnd": 2215,
      "sha256": "decc475be5e27a64f364370b3d4b0ef3bb1d7b67cc9463f669a8a05a4ebf1eb1"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "la-ch09-item-1-2-2",
      "sourceStart": 2215,
      "sourceEnd": 2357,
      "original": "#### 1.2.2. <span style=\"color: red\">(Permutation)</span> Let $S_m$ denote the permutations of $\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, and let $\\sigma\\in S_m$. Then",
      "action": "moved-to-item-title-and-statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "blockId": "la-ch09-block-0009",
      "sourceStart": 2357,
      "sourceEnd": 2557,
      "sha256": "c1311e1197c8a1bcb042c39142aaff15be6057f8387e68a6e3f946430b779599"
    },
    {
